 ### Added
 
-- `SessionStore.listFor(userId, options?)`, an optional store member that answers the summaries of a user's conversations — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount` — most recent activity first, so a consumer can build a conversation list instead of only rejoining one. Nothing in the engine calls it yet: the store answers the list, and the route or frame that carries it to a client is still yours to write. `InMemorySessionStore` and `MongoSessionStore` both implement it; a store of your own may leave it out. A summary never carries a conversation's entries or its credentials. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most, and `0` answers nothing) and `before`, which takes the last summary you saw: the cursor carries both `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. See `docs/session-stores.md` § Listing a user's conversations.
+- The engine now answers a conversation list on the WebSocket itself, so a client can offer a history picker without the consumer standing up an authenticated route of their own over the same data. Send `{"type": "list_sessions"}` — optionally with `limit` (a whole number from 0 up) and a `before` cursor of `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, both as the store takes them — and the engine answers with one `{"type": "session_list", "sessions": [...]}` frame, newest activity first, each row carrying `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount`, the two times as ISO strings since a `Date` does not survive JSON. The list is always the connecting user's own: the user id comes from the authenticated connection, and a `userId` on the frame is ignored, so one socket can never read another user's history. The frame is answered off the per-session turn lock, beside `ping`, so a history request never queues behind a streaming answer. A store that implements no `listFor` is answered with an `error` frame (`UNSUPPORTED`) rather than an empty list, since an empty history is not a safe guess for a user whose conversations the same store serves to resume; one that fails is answered with an `error` frame (`SERVER_ERROR`) and the failure logged without any conversation content. See `specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md` for the two frames.
+- `SessionStore.listFor(userId, options?)`, an optional store member that answers the summaries of a user's conversations — `sessionId`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt` and `entryCount` — most recent activity first, so a consumer can build a conversation list instead of only rejoining one. `InMemorySessionStore` and `MongoSessionStore` both implement it; a store of your own may leave it out. A summary never carries a conversation's entries or its credentials. Page with `limit` (50 by default, 200 at most, and `0` answers nothing) and `before`, which takes the last summary you saw: the cursor carries both `updatedAt` and `sessionId`, so two conversations saved in the same millisecond cannot straddle a page boundary and go unlisted. See `docs/session-stores.md` § Listing a user's conversations.
 
